+// Test that return type qualifiers of function calls are used as assumptions.
+
+def double(x: Int): {res: Int with res == x + x} = x + x
+
+// The solver should use double's return type to prove double(3) == 6.
+val d: {res: Int with res == 6} = double(3)
+
+// Chained: the result of one qualified call used in another.
+def inc(x: Int): {res: Int with res == x + 1} = x + 1
+val v: {res: Int with res == 4} = inc(3)
+val w: {res: Int with res == 5} = inc(inc(3))

From 6fa3cdb1a075e469e2cd2ce359cf1c12c2874268 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anonymous <anonymous@example.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Mar 2026 11:45:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 52/56] Display `ENodeParamRef` with nice names
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Instead of showing raw "eparam0", "eparam1" etc., ENodeParamRef values
are now displayed as "x" with superscript disambiguation (x, x², x³...)
in error messages, with a "where" clause explaining each one. The
disambiguation correctly identifies same-parameter references across
different lambda nesting depths using binding levels (depth - index).
Lambda parameter declarations also use the disambiguated names instead
of underscores.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
